## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical data quality, late data handling, and observability in a real-world lakehouse pipeline.

## Key Concepts
- Per-record contracts and simple validator logic
- Upserts into Iceberg star schema with deduplication by event_id
- Late data handling with watermarking (15 minutes)
- Lightweight observability: lineage, conforming vs non-conforming counts, metrics sink
- Trade-offs: Spark Structured Streaming vs Flink for beginner workloads

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo validator
function validate(record) {
  if (!record.event_id || !record.ts || !record.device_id) return false;
  if (typeof record.metrics?.value !== 'number') return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you design the bad_data retention and purge policy?
- Which metrics would you expose to operators for quick health checks?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your ability to design scalable, multi-tenant data pipelines that handle schema diversity, guarantee data quality, and maintain operational reliability under high throughput.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant schema registry and validation
- Idempotent upserts using Iceberg MERGE operations
- Late data handling with time-partitioned snapshots
- Data quality enforcement via JSON Schema and Great Expectations
- Observability through comprehensive monitoring and automated rollback testing

## Code Example
```javascript
function upsertEvent(table, event) {
  // Validate against tenant-specific schema
  const isValid = validateEvent(event.tenant_id, event);
  if (!isValid) throw new Error('Schema validation failed');

  // Perform idempotent upsert using MERGE
  return table.merge()
    .whenMatched().updateAll()
    .whenNotMatched().insertAll()
    .execute();
}
```
